faq:
  - q: "How do I prepare for a technical interview?"
    a: "Six components: (1) Coding fundamentals — arrays, strings, trees, graphs, dynamic programming, sorting. Practice on LeetCode or similar (150–200 problems minimum). (2) System design — practice designing distributed systems at scale: design Twitter, design a URL shortener, design a real-time notification system. Use a structured approach: clarify requirements, estimate scale, define data model, design API, design system components. (3) Behavioral prep — prepare 6–8 STAR stories covering technical decisions, cross-functional influence, and execution under pressure. (4) Company research — understand the company's tech stack, architecture decisions, and engineering culture. (5) Role calibration — know what level you are interviewing for. Staff-level system design differs significantly from senior-level. (6) Mock practice — do at least 3 full mock interviews before your real loop."
  - q: "How long does it take to prepare for a technical interview?"
    a: "For a standard senior software engineer loop: 4–8 weeks of consistent daily practice (1–2 hours per day). For Staff or Principal level: 8–12 weeks. For candidates who have not practiced algorithms recently, starting with fundamentals takes 2–3 weeks before advancing to harder problems."
  - q: "What is the most common technical interview mistake?"
    a: "Not communicating while solving. Interviewers evaluate your thinking process, not just your solution. Talking through your approach, stating assumptions, discussing tradeoffs, and explaining why you chose one approach over another is often weighted as heavily as the correctness of the solution. Silent coding is the most common and most costly mistake."
  - q: "How do I prepare for system design interviews?"
    a: "Use a consistent framework: (1) Clarify requirements and constraints. (2) Estimate scale (users, requests per second, data volume). (3) Define the data model. (4) Design the API. (5) Draw the high-level architecture. (6) Deep-dive on one component. (7) Discuss tradeoffs and failure modes. Practice 8–10 common designs (URL shortener, notification system, rate limiter, distributed cache, news feed) and you will have the patterns for almost any design question."
  - q: "What is the difference between senior and staff-level technical interviews?"
    a: "Senior interviews focus on: solving problems correctly, communicating approach, knowing standard data structures. Staff interviews focus on: system design at scale, architectural tradeoffs, ambiguity navigation, cross-team impact, and mentorship signal. Staff candidates are expected to drive the conversation, not just answer questions."
howto:
  name: "How to Prepare for a Technical Interview"
  steps:
    - name: "Audit your starting point"
      text: "Before building a study plan, assess where you are. If you have not practiced algorithms in over a year, start with fundamentals — arrays, strings, hashmaps — before moving to dynamic programming or graphs. If your system design is weak, that is where to invest first for Staff-level loops. Know your gaps before scheduling interviews."
    - name: "Build your coding problem reps"
      text: "Target 150–200 LeetCode problems minimum. Prioritize mediums. Cover: arrays and strings, two pointers, sliding window, binary search, trees and graphs (BFS/DFS), dynamic programming, recursion and backtracking, heaps and priority queues. Time each session — simulate interview pace from the start."
    - name: "Master the system design framework"
      text: "For every design question: clarify requirements and constraints first, estimate scale (users, RPS, data volume), define the data model, design the API, draw the high-level architecture, deep-dive one component, then discuss tradeoffs and failure modes. Practice 8–10 canonical designs until the framework is automatic."
    - name: "Prepare behavioral stories for technical loops"
      text: "Most technical loops include at least one behavioral round. Prepare 6–8 STAR stories covering: a major technical decision and its tradeoffs, a time you influenced a non-technical stakeholder, a time you dealt with technical debt under pressure, and your highest-impact engineering project. These stories should answer both engineering and leadership questions."
    - name: "Run at least 3 full mock interviews"
      text: "Mock interviews under real conditions — timed, spoken aloud, with feedback — are different from solo practice. Do at least 3 full mock loops before your real interview. Include coding, system design, and behavioral rounds. Get specific feedback on your communication, not just your solutions."
